U.S. Geological Survey, ANSS Comprehensive Earthquake Catalog (ComCat)
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Dataset: earthquakes (67,510 earthquakes of magnitude 5 or more, 1986 to 2025)

Licence: U.S. Public Domain, recorded as CC-PDDC. The USGS states:
"USGS-authored or produced data and information are considered to be in
the U.S. Public Domain."
https://www.usgs.gov/information-policies-and-instructions/copyrights-and-credits
(read 2026-10-12)

Credit, as the same page asks for it: "Credit: U.S. Geological Survey"

Where and when: the USGS FDSN event service, one query per calendar year
from 1986 to 2025, read on 2026-10-12 between 11:37:40 and 11:38:40 UTC:

  https://earthquake.usgs.gov/fdsnws/event/1/query?format=csv&starttime=<year>-01-01T00:00:00&endtime=<year>-12-31T23:59:59.999&minmagnitude=5&eventtype=earthquake&orderby=time-asc

Kept: the event id, time (UTC), magnitude and its type, depth, and place;
depth sorted into three classes (under 70 km, 70 to 300 km, 300 km or
more). The catalogue is revised as solutions are reviewed, so a build on
another day can differ by a few events.


World tectonic plates and boundaries (PB2002)
---------------------------------------------

Dataset: plate_boundaries (241 boundary lines)

Licence: Open Data Commons Attribution License v1.0 (ODC-By-1.0).
https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/by/1-0/

Credit, as the publisher asks for it: "Please consider giving Hugo
Ahlenius, Nordpil and Peter Bird credit and recognition as a data source."

The original model: Bird, P. (2003), An updated digital model of plate
boundaries, Geochemistry Geophysics Geosystems, 4(3), 1027,
doi:10.1029/2001GC000252. Converted to GIS formats by Hugo Ahlenius,
Nordpil; GeoJSON by csterling.

Where and when: read on 2026-10-12 at 11:38:41 UTC from the publisher's
repository at a pinned commit:

  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/fraxen/tectonicplates/339b0c56563c118307b1f4542703047f5f698fae/GeoJSON/PB2002_boundaries.json
  (226,378 bytes, SHA-256 42b3e0876a7e40f133e958ba7ab85f8851b5c693a046fbc3b138e7751863d92a)

Kept: the boundary name, its two plates and the source of each segment;
the boundary type read as "Subduction zone" or "Other plate boundary".


